54
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:02,600
Another cup of tea, Paul?

55
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:05,560
No, thanks. Well, what's new, Laurie?

56
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:09,840
Nothing dramatic, but there's something you should know about.

57
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:15,220
What's that? Well, our rivals are offering extended credit terms to some of the

58
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:17,180
retailers in the Oh,

59
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:19,160
which rivals this?

60
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:20,540
only have two.

61
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,140
Barrett's company. Oh, them.

62
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:25,060
Well, they're hardly a threat.

63
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:29,120
I know they're smaller than us, but we can't afford to ignore them.

64
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:30,840
Yes, you're right, Laurie.

65
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:33,280
But I don't like extended credit.

66
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:36,500
It ties up cash we could put to better use elsewhere.

67
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:38,960
But I'll look into it on Monday.

68
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:41,320
Yes, and there's something else.

69
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:42,920
Don't tell me.

70
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:45,160
The letter from the tax revenue office?

71
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:47,200
Right. did you know?

72
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:48,860
Tara told me.

73
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:49,780
What's the problem?

74
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:56,220
Well, Tom got this letter late yesterday and then went frantic trying to find copies

75
00:04:56,220 --> 00:04:57,880
of last year's accounts.

76
00:04:58,440 --> 00:04:59,300
Did he find them?

77
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:03,000
No, and he was way before I could get hold of the letter.

78
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:06,060
How about a drive down to the office now?

79
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:07,740
And we'll see if everything's all right.

80
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:11,620
There's another reason why I wanted to chat with you before Monday.

81
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:13,140
I thought as much.

82
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:15,740
go on. me.

83
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:19,580
about selling that new motorcycle of yours in Indonesia?

84
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:22,200
What? You mean export?

85
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:25,080
Paul, I think you've been away too long.

86
00:05:25,620 --> 00:05:27,080
This is jail motors.

87
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:29,720
We've never sold a bike abroad.

88
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:31,480
Don't worry, Laurie.

89
00:05:31,940 --> 00:05:32,840
I'm not crazy.

90
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:37,160
I've been studying the possibility and I think we should give it a go.

91
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:39,760
It's not as easy as that though, is it?

92
00:05:39,920 --> 00:05:42,420
We'd have to reorganize the whole company.

93
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:44,100
Don't be silly.

94
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:46,740
I don't intend starting next week.

95
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:48,440
We'll have to plan it properly.

96
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:50,560
And of course, there'll be a few problems.

97
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:51,840
A few problems?

98
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:53,580
I can see hundreds.

99
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:55,340
For one thing, transport.

100
00:05:56,040 --> 00:06:01,000
I have enough trouble delivering bikes to shops only 40 miles away, never mind 5,000

101
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:04,720
miles. That's what forwarding agents are for.

