00:18:24,120 --> 00:18:31,500
Recording 2. Stereotype may sound like a bad word, but there's nothing bad about it.

218
00:18:31,820 --> 00:18:35,260
For one thing, stereotypes are often accurate.

219
00:18:35,940 --> 00:18:41,140
When you ask people about their concept of stereotypes, they get it pretty much

220
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:47,980
right. Also, stereotypes are often positive, particularly of groups that we

221
00:18:47,980 --> 00:18:49,540
ourselves belong to.

222
00:18:50,200 --> 00:18:55,600
Some of the statistical generalisations may be positive, as some groups have

223
00:18:55,600 --> 00:18:59,020
reputations for being smart, for being loyal,

224
00:18:59,760 --> 00:19:06,340
for being brave, for all sorts of that are

225
00:19:07,240 --> 00:19:09,280
wrong about stereotypes.

226
00:19:10,360 --> 00:19:12,720
But there are problems with stereotypes.

227
00:19:13,380 --> 00:19:19,020
For one thing, they are reliable insofar as they are based on unbiased samples.

228
00:19:20,320 --> 00:19:26,300
But a lot of the information we get about human groups is through biased sources,

229
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:28,960
like how they are represented in the media.

230
00:19:29,900 --> 00:19:36,320
And if these sources don't give you an accurate depiction, your stereotype won't be

231
00:19:36,320 --> 00:19:42,540
accurate. For example, many Jews have been troubled by Shakespeare's depiction of

232
00:19:42,540 --> 00:19:48,420
Shylock. If the only Jew you know is Shakespeare's Shylock, it's going to be a very

233
00:19:48,420 --> 00:19:54,120
bad impression. So one problem with stereotypes is while we're good at drawing

234
00:19:54,120 --> 00:19:59,700
conclusions from them, often our information isn't reliable.

235
00:20:00,100 --> 00:20:05,120
second problem is that stereotypes, regardless of whether or not they're accurate,

236
00:20:05,860 --> 00:20:09,920
can have a negative effect on the people that they apply to.

237
00:20:10,400 --> 00:20:15,540
And this is what psychologist Claude Steele described as stereotype threat.

238
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:18,960
He has a vivid example of this.

239
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:24,200
Here's how to make African Americans do worse on a math test.

240
00:20:24,840 --> 00:20:30,460
You have the test and you put on the test that they have to identify their race.

241
00:20:31,460 --> 00:20:36,780
The very act of acknowledging that they're African American when given a test

242
00:20:36,780 --> 00:20:39,640
ignites in them thoughts of their own stereotype,

243
00:20:40,380 --> 00:20:45,380
which is negative regarding academics, and that makes them do worse.

